  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on survey data from a specific rural patient population in Illinois, which may not generalize to urban NEMT users or other geographic regions.",
      "what": "This study analyzes how user perceptions of non-emergency medical transportation (NEMT) service quality affect service satisfaction and subjective well-being. Using survey data from rural patients in Illinois, the analysis evaluates the influence of reliability, reservation technology, customer service, accessibility, and driver behavior.",
      "why": "It establishes a direct link between specialized medical transit quality and the subjective well-being of vulnerable rural populations, identifying service reliability as the primary driver of patient satisfaction."
